## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of privacy-first telemetry with per-tenant policies, plus practical exposure to a lightweight streaming path and simple data governance.

## Key Concepts

- Ingestion-time redaction and policy evaluation
- Per-tenant governance and allow/drop decisions
- Region-based streaming topology and partitioned storage
- Dashboard freshness and SLAs

## Code Example

```javascript
function redactPayload(tenantId, payload, policyMap){
  const policy = policyMap[tenantId] || {};
  const redacted = {...payload};
  if(policy.redactPII){
    if('device_id' in redacted) redacted.device_id = 'REDACTED';
    if('location' in redacted) redacted.location = 'REDACTED';
  }
  if(policy.allowedEvents && !policy.allowedEvents.includes(payload.event_type)){
    return null; // drop
  }
  return redacted;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate policy drift and compliance over time?
- How would you evolve the schema for new event types without breaking dashboards?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of schema evolution, per-tenant isolation, versioned data contracts, and end-to-end visibility under partial deployments.

## Key Concepts

- Versioned schemas with schema_version
- Backward/forward compatibility
- Ingest-time shims/transformations
- Region routing by tenant
- Privacy controls

## Code Example

```javascript
function normalizeEvent(raw) {
  const v = raw.schema_version ?? 1;
  if (v === 1) {
    return { tenant_id: raw.tenant_id, event_type: raw.event_type, timestamp: raw.timestamp, payload: raw.payload, session_id: null, latency_ms: null, schema_version: 2 };
  }
  return raw;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test for backward compatibility when a deployed app carries mixed-v1 and v2 events?
- What are trade-offs of adding latency_ms at ingestion vs computing it at source?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical OTCA telemetry design for AI inference with explainability, focusing on tenancy, privacy, and compatibility rather than volume. It requires mapping events, streaming paths, retention, and a lightweight schema-registry and tests, while introducing explainability data as a new angle.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-tenant AI telemetry
- Privacy-preserving ingestion (hashing, redaction)
- Per-tenant residency and retention
- Schema registry compatibility
- Basic QoS testing

## Code Example
```javascript
function redact(input){
  const crypto = require('crypto');
  const h = crypto.createHash('sha256');
  h.update(String(input));
  return h.digest('hex');
}
```

